#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand snap revisions, the unique identifiers assigned to snap uploads, "
"including how to view them, how they are installed, and how to revert them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:8
msgid "Revisions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:10
msgid ""
"A snap's *revision* is a number assigned automatically by the {ref}`Snap "
"Store <ref-glossary_snap-store>` with each snap upload, giving each snap "
"binary a unique identity within, and across, its {ref}`channels <explanation-"
"how-snaps-work-channels-and-tracks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:12
msgid ""
"The revision number increments with each new upload. But this number is "
"arbitrary, and only used to differentiate between uploads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:14
msgid ""
"Neither the revision number (nor its version) enforce an order of release. "
"The local system will attempt to install whatever snap is recommended by the "
"publisher in the channel being tracked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:16
msgid "Viewing revision numbers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:18
msgid ""
"The output to `snap info <snapname>` includes the revision number for each "
"snap in each track and channel as a number in brackets after the publishing "
"date:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:20
msgid ""
"channels:\n"
"  latest/stable:    2.59.4                 2023-05-31 (19361) 55MB -\n"
"  latest/candidate: 2.59.5                 2023-06-09 (19457) 55MB -\n"
"  latest/beta:      2.59.5                 2023-05-27 (19457) 55MB -\n"
"  latest/edge:      2.59.5+git955.gc9310cc 2023-06-13 (19535) 42MB -\n"
"installed:          2.59.1+git798.g5f761c7            (19131) 42MB snapd\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:29
msgid ""
"In the above example output, the latest/edge snap has a revision of `19535` "
"and is the most recent published revision of the snap. If the snap is "
"installed, the final line of `snap info` output includes the installed "
"revision (`19131`, above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:31
msgid ""
"The `snap list` command includes a column for each installed snap's revision "
"number, labelled `Rev`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:33
msgid ""
"Name         Version  Rev    Tracking       Publisher             Notes\n"
"alacritty    0.8.0    46     latest/stable  snapcrafters✪         classic\n"
"blender      3.5.0    3486   latest/stable  blenderfoundation✓    classic\n"
"chromium     112.0    2424   latest/stable  canonical✓            -\n"
"ffmpeg       4.3.1    1286   latest/stable  snapcrafters✪         -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:41
msgid "Revision package management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:43
msgid ""
"The store caches several older revisions of every snap, as does the local "
"system. By default, 2 revisions are stored locally, while Ubuntu Core "
"systems store 3. These defaults can be changed with the {ref}`refresh-retain "
"<how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-manage-updates>` system option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:45
msgid ""
"The snap `install`, `refresh` and `download` commands can operate on these "
"available revisions with an optional `--revision` argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:47
msgid "snap <command> <snap-name> --revision <revision-number>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:51
msgid ""
"To install revision number _27_ of the hello-world snap, for example, run "
"the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:53
msgid ""
"$ snap install hello-world --revision 27\n"
"hello-world 6.3 from Canonical✓ installed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:58
msgid ""
"The hello-world snap could be refreshed to revision number _28_ with the "
"following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:60
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh hello-world --revision 28\n"
"hello-world 6.3 from Canonical✓ refreshed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:65
msgid ""
"The revision number of the snap being operated upon will appear in the "
"output during these operations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:67
msgid ""
"[Manage revisions and releases](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/"
"latest/how-to/publishing/manage-revisions-and-releases/) details how a snap "
"developer can publish or promote specific revisions of their snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:69
msgid "Data management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:71
msgid ""
"The revision identity is used as a reference for revision-specific data. As "
"described in {ref}`Data locations <interfaces-data-locations>`, revision-"
"specific data for a snap is stored in a either system-wide location, or a "
"user-specific home location:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:73
msgid ""
"**SNAP_ DATA**: `/var/snap/<snap name>/<revision number>`</br> This location "
"is also used to store data, mostly information utilised by background "
"application and services, for logging, and other tasks that require "
"persistence between snap launches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:73
#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:75
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:75
msgid ""
"**SNAP_USER_DATA** : `/home/<username>/snap/<snap name>/<revision>`</br> "
"This location contains any user data that the snap writes to its own home. "
"This is *in contrast* to what the Linux user would consider *their* home, "
"although the location itself will be in the user's home directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:78
msgid ""
"It is important to note this distinction, because it can be useful, and even "
"important when users decide to perform maintenance operations with their "
"snaps (like removal). By default, every snap will use a symlink *current* , "
"pointing to the latest available revision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:80
msgid ""
"When you move from one revision to the next, the revision-specific contents "
"of **SNAP_DATA** and **SNAP_USER_DATA** are copied into new directories for "
"the new revision. This includes moving from a higher revision number to a "
"lower revision number (because revision numbers are arbitrary)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:82
msgid ""
"Revision-specific directories are retained inline with the {ref}`refresh-"
"retain <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-manage-updates>` system option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/revisions.md:84
msgid ""
"Other than the contents of the common directories, a {ref}`Snapshot <how-to-"
"guides-manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` stores only the data associated "
"with the currently installed revision. See {ref}`What a snapshot stores <ref-"
"create-data-snapshots_what-a-snapshot-stores>` for more details."
msgstr ""
